🧵 Thread Life Cycle in Java (For Beginners)
A Java thread goes through 6 stages during its life.
Think of a thread as a person doing a task — it has different states like "Born", "Ready", "Working", "Waiting", etc.

⭐ 1. NEW (Born State)
Thread object is created, but not started yet.

Thread t = new Thread();
Thread exists but is not running.

⭐ 2. RUNNABLE (Ready to Run + Running)
When we call start(), the thread becomes runnable.

t.start();
This means the thread is ready to run, and JVM scheduler will run it when CPU is available.

👉 In Java, Runnable = Running + Ready state combined.

⭐ 3. RUNNING (Actively executing)
JVM gives CPU time, and the thread is executing its run() method.

You don't control this directly.
JVM scheduler decides which thread runs when.

⭐ 4. BLOCKED (Waiting for a lock)
Thread wants to enter a synchronized block/method but another thread is using it.

Example:

synchronized(obj) {
    // Only one thread can enter here at a time
}
Thread becomes BLOCKED until the lock is released.

⭐ 5. WAITING / TIMED_WAITING (Waiting State)
Thread is not using CPU — it is waiting because:

👉 WAITING
wait()

join()

park()

Thread waits for another thread to signal.

👉 TIMED_WAITING
sleep(5000)

wait(2000)

join(5000)

parkNanos()

Thread waits for a specific time.

⭐ 6. TERMINATED (Dead State)
Thread’s run() method has finished.

Thread cannot be restarted again.

System.out.println("Thread finished");
🧵 Visual Diagram of Thread Lifecycle
 NEW → RUNNABLE → RUNNING
           ↑          |
           |          ↓
         BLOCKED ← WAITING / TIMED_WAITING
           ↓
      TERMINATED
🧠 Example illustrating states
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        try {
            System.out.println("Thread running...");
            Thread.sleep(2000); // TIMED_WAITING
            System.out.println("Thread finishing...");
        } catch (Exception e) {}
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread(); // NEW
        t.start();                    // RUNNABLE
    }
}
States:
NEW → when created

RUNNABLE → after start()

RUNNING → when run() executes

TIMED_WAITING → during sleep(2000)

TERMINATED → when run() ends

🎯 Summary Table
State	Meaning
NEW	Thread created but not started
RUNNABLE	Ready to run / Running
RUNNING	JVM executing the thread
BLOCKED	Waiting for lock
WAITING	Waiting indefinitely
TIMED_WAITING	Waiting for fixed time
TERMINATED	Finished
