🔒 What is Synchronization in Multithreading? (For Beginners)
👉 Simple Definition
Synchronization means controlling access to shared resources so that only one thread uses it at a time.

It prevents problems when multiple threads try to modify the same data at the same time.

🧠 Real-life example (Easy to understand)
Imagine there is:

One ATM machine (shared resource)

Two people trying to withdraw money (two threads)

If both try at the same time → data conflict → wrong balance.

So ATM allows only one person at a time.
This is synchronization.

🧠 In Java, shared resources can be:
Variables

Objects

Files

Database

Critical code sections

If two threads access them together, the program can produce wrong results or crash.

🔥 Why Synchronization?
To avoid:

Incorrect output

Data corruption

Thread interference

Race conditions

🔧 How Java Provides Synchronization?
Java uses locks to make sure only one thread enters a critical section.

Java provides:
synchronized method

synchronized block

Intrinsic locks (object-level lock)

Static lock (class-level lock)

